## 🎛️ Phase 2: SIEM Detection Engineering & False-Positive Tuning
**Goal:** Suppress benign false positives observed during Guide 04 (such as `Rule 61640` firing on legitimate `explorer.exe` activity) without blinding real detections.

1. **Inspect the Benign Alert (`Rule 61640`):**
   * In Wazuh **Threat Hunting**, expand one of the `Rule 61640` (*Sysmon - Suspicious Process - explorer.exe*) events and note the legitimate parent process or image path (e.g., `C:\Windows\explorer.exe`).
2. **Add a Tuning Exception in `local_rules.xml` (Wazuh Manager):**
   * SSH into your Wazuh server and edit `/var/ossec/etc/rules/local_rules.xml`:
     ```bash
     sudo nano /var/ossec/etc/rules/local_rules.xml
     ```
   * Add a targeted child rule lowering the level to `0` (ignore) **only** when legitimate `C:\Windows\explorer.exe` is launched by `userinit.exe`:
     ```xml
     <group name="sysmon,tuning,">
       <rule id="100105" level="0">
         <if_sid>61640</if_sid>
         <field name="win.eventdata.image">^C:\\Windows\\explorer.exe\$</field>
         <field name="win.eventdata.parentImage">^C:\\Windows\\System32\\userinit.exe\$</field>
         <description>Ignore benign Windows Explorer userinit startup event.</description>
       </rule>
     </group>
     ```
3. **Validate & Restart Wazuh Manager:**
   ```bash
   sudo /var/ossec/bin/wazuh-logtest
   sudo systemctl restart wazuh-manager
   ```

